From 1171d434ffa3024c538ee62bc34b697cf87b18de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 22:02:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Count where the boxes are actually minted, item 3-1

The guarded tree removed 12.2% of the corpus's boxes and left NavierStokes --
the biggest boxer in it -- down 1.8%, so the question is where the remaining
74.8 M are minted. The reading that suggests itself is that they are root
boxes on their way into a JSValue slot or element, which is what a typed
backing store would remove. Give the compiler's boxing conversion its own
factory entry and it is refutable: JSNumber.CreateConversion, counted apart
from Create and CreateLiteral exactly as the literal entry already is, with
JSNumberBuilder.New pointing at it.

It is 18.4% of the corpus's requests, and 5.0% of NavierStokes' against 31.0%
of Crypto's -- the two suites the opposite way round from the item's premise,
and Crypto is the one the guarded tree already served best.

That first pass left 40.5% of the corpus's requests attributed to nothing at
all, which is a claim about the census rather than about the engine. Two
counters close it to 1.0%. BitwiseXor was the one generic binary operator the
census never hooked -- a real gap, though the corpus says a small one, below
the run-to-run spread. The rest is the unary operators, which no census had
looked at: -x and ~x, the ++/-- step, and the ToNumeric that coerces the
operand of ++/--.

++ and -- are 30.9% of all boxing on the corpus, 51.6% on NavierStokes and
80.4% on EarleyBoyer -- more than the compiler conversion and the numeric
literal together. Exactly half of it is waste: ToNumeric ends

    primitive.IsBigInt ? primitive : CreateNumber(primitive.DoubleValue)

so an operand that is already a JSNumber is copied into a second, equal
JSNumber to be handed back as the old value, and a Number has no observable
identity. 17 281 232 requests, 15.4% of the corpus's boxing, for a value the
engine is already holding.

Four new fixtures. The three that assert on the unary columns are
deliberately loop-free, and the reason is the finding in miniature: written
with the `for (var i = 0; i < 10; i++)` the rest of the file uses, all three
failed, because the loop counter's own i++ lands in the columns they assert
on.

Counters off by default, no behaviour change. Full repository suite 7 967
tests, 0 failures.
